WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.420
Moodle authorization is built from four related ideas: capabilities, roles, contexts, and assignments.

2
00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:12.840
A capability is a permission to do a specific kind of action.

3
00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:16.700
A role is a named collection of capabilities.

4
00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:24.960
A context is the scope where that role applies, such as the system, a category, a course, an activity, or a block.

5
00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:28.420
A role assignment connects a user to a role in a context.

6
00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:32.280
Good administration starts by keeping those pieces separate in your mind.

7
00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:36.720
A common mistake is to treat a role name as a universal job description.

8
00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:45.060
The same person might teach one course, manage another course category, review reports in a limited area, and have no administrative rights elsewhere.

9
00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:51.560
Moodle can support that nuance, but only if administrators assign roles at the right context.

10
00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:55.100
A system-level assignment travels widely and should be rare.

11
00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:59.440
A course-level or category-level assignment may satisfy the need with less risk.

12
00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:04.120
Least privilege is not distrust; it is operational hygiene.

13
00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:09.820
People should receive enough access to complete their work, for the period they need it, in the place they need it.

14
00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:18.320
Excess permissions can expose personal data, alter grades, change course content, affect enrolments, or hide the source of a mistake.

15
00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:25.220
In learning environments, over-permissioning can also undermine academic integrity and confidence in records.

16
00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:27.580
Custom roles require discipline.

17
00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:40.320
It may be reasonable to create a support role, auditor role, category manager role, or limited reporting role, but every customization should have a purpose, owner, test case, and review date.

18
00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:46.180
Copying a powerful role and removing a few obvious capabilities can still leave broad authority.

19
00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:53.320
Administrators should compare custom roles against Moodle defaults and verify behavior using test accounts in the intended contexts.

20
00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:55.840
Temporary elevation needs a lifecycle.

21
00:01:55.940 --> 00:02:05.220
Emergency access, implementation access, and vendor support access should be approved, time-limited, documented, and removed when no longer needed.

22
00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:12.520
Shared administrator accounts make accountability weaker and should not become the normal answer to operational pressure.

23
00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:17.340
A backup administrator is useful; uncontrolled standing access is a different thing.

24
00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:20.900
Permissions decisions connect directly to governance.

25
00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:29.720
They determine who can view data, change content, affect assessment records, manage users, install plugins, and interpret evidence.

26
00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:36.700
A reliable Moodle site has permission records that explain why access exists, not just who currently has it.

27
00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:45.600
When roles, capabilities, and contexts are governed well, the platform becomes easier to support, audit, and recover.
